[Line "Tal Variation: 4.h4 h5 5.Bd3"]
[Section "3...Bf5 4.h4: the Tal Variation (second weapon)"]
[Summary "After ...h5 White exchanges bishops after all, puts the other bishop on g5 and opens the centre with c4. The g5-square and the pawn on h5 are permanent assets."]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 Bf5 4. h4 { [%cal Gg2g4,Gh4h5] The Tal Variation, and Shaw's repertoire choice. The
threat is g4 followed by h5, winning the bishop, so Black must do something about the h-pawn at once. }
4... h5 { The main move: it stops g4 for good. The price is the g5-square and a target on h5. }
( 4... e6?? { The natural developing move loses a piece. } 5. g4 Be4 6. f3 Bg6 7. h5 { The bishop has no
square: h7 is occupied, and g4 and f3 cover the rest. } )
5. Bd3 { [%cal Gc1g5] In the Short System this exchange was a mistake. Here it is the modern main line, because
the pawn moves have changed the balance: Black can never play ...h6, so g5 belongs to White's bishop, the pawn
on h5 needs constant protection, and the queen on d3 looks at g6. }
( 5. c4 { The older main line, and the one Shaw gives in Playing 1.e4: White keeps the bishops and opens the
c-file. Black chooses between 6...Ne7 followed by ...Nd7, and 6...dxc4 7.Bxc4 Nd7 (Eljanov), playing for the
d5-square. A good alternative if you prefer more pieces on the board. } 5... e6 6. Nc3 )
5... Bxd3 6. Qxd3 e6
( 6... Qa5+ { The move Eljanov's repertoire for Black calls essential: before ...e6 the queen checks and heads
for a6 to offer an exchange. } 7. Nd2 { 7.b4 is a serious pawn sacrifice (7...Qxb4+ 8.Nd2 e6 9.Rb1 Qe7 10.c4),
for those who want more. } 7... e6 8. Ngf3 Nh6 9. O-O Nf5 10. Nb3 Qa6 11. Qd1 { White keeps the queens on.
Eljanov considers 11...b6 followed by ...c5 satisfactory for Black. The position is balanced, but Black still
has to solve the problem of the king: castling short leaves h5 weak, and Bg5 is always available. } )
7. Bg5 { [%csl Yg5] The point of exchanging on d3. The bishop sits outside its pawn chain on a square from which
no pawn can drive it, and it cramps the whole kingside: ...Ne7 walks into a pin, ...Be7 offers Black's better
bishop for exchange, and ...f6 weakens e6 and g6. }
7... Qb6 { The main reply: the queen leaves the bishop's diagonal, attacks b2 and prepares ...Qa6. }
( 7... Be7 { Black accepts the exchange of dark-squared bishops to free the position. } 8. Nf3 Nh6 9. Nbd2
{ White does not hurry to take on e7. After the exchange the d6-square and the pawn on h5 stay weak, and
Bxh6 is an extra option now that the knight has gone there. } )
8. Nd2 { The b-pawn is offered: after 8...Qxb2 9.Rb1 and 10.Rxb7 the rook is on the seventh rank before Black
has developed a kingside piece. The knight supports c4 and will be followed by the other one on f3. }
8... Qa6 { Black offers queens to get rid of the pressure on the light squares. }
( 8... c5 { The other main move. } 9. c4 { The same break. With both c-pawns advanced the centre is blown open
while Black's kingside is still at home. The usual reply, 9...Qxb2, leads to wild play after 10.Rd1 or
10.Rb1. } )
9. c4 { [%cal Gd2c4] White refuses, since after Qxa6 Nxa6 Black's game is easy, and uses the queen's position to
open the centre. If Black takes, Nxc4 brings the knight towards d6. }
9... Nd7 10. Ngf3 Ne7 11. O-O { **Plan:** rooks to the c- and d-files, then cxd5 or c5 according to where
Black's king goes. A knight heading for f4 attacks h5 and e6.

**Verdict:** White is the more comfortable: more space, the better minor pieces, and a weakness on h5 that will
still be there in the ending. This is the line that has made 4.h4 the most popular fourth move in elite online
play. } *

[Line "Tal Variation: 4.h4 h6 5.g4"]
[Section "3...Bf5 4.h4: the Tal Variation (second weapon)"]
[Summary "Black keeps g5 covered but lets White gain space with tempo. The bishop either retreats to d7, with a French where White owns the kingside, or allows the pawn sacrifice e6."]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 Bf5 4. h4 h6 { Black gives the bishop a retreat on h7 and keeps g5 under control. The
cost is that g4 now comes with tempo. } 5. g4 { White takes the space for nothing. The bishop has three squares,
and only the most modest one avoids the e6 sacrifice. }
5... Bd7 { Played by Leko and Ivanchuk (Silman). Black admits that the bishop went to f5 for nothing and plays a
French with ...c5, counting on White's advanced kingside pawns to become targets. }
( 5... Be4 { Provoking f3 first, which takes that square from the knight. } 6. f3 Bh7 7. e6! { [%csl Rg6,Re6] The
thematic sacrifice. If Black takes, the e6-pawn blocks the f8-bishop and the whole kingside, and Bd3 exchanges
the only piece that covers g6. } 7... Qd6 { The usual reply, attacking the pawn and threatening ...Qg3+. }
8. exf7+ Kxf7 9. f4 { Closes the diagonal to g3 and takes e5 for the knight. Material is level, Black's king has
lost the right to castle and the bishop on h7 is shut out; Nf3-e5+ and Bd3 follow. } )
( 5... Bh7 { The retreat the bishop was given h7 for. } 6. e6! { The same idea without f3 included. }
6... fxe6 7. Bd3 { White is a pawn down for a lasting bind: after the exchange of bishops the queen comes to
g6 with check, the king has to stay in the centre, and Nf3-e5 follows. } )
6. h5 { [%csl Rg7] Fixes the kingside pawns and, above all, stops Black from ever undermining g4 with ...h5.
The same move is right against 4...Qb6 for the same reason. }
6... e6 7. f4 c5 8. c3 Nc6 9. Nf3 { **Plan:** a French Advance in which Black's bishop is back on d7 and White
has been given g4, h5 and f4 for nothing. White defends d4 (Na3-c2, with Rh2 to cover b2 after ...Qb6), puts
the king on f2 and prepares f5.

**Verdict:** more space and the initiative for White. The thing to respect is White's own king, which has no
pawn cover: do not let ...Qb6 and ...cxd4 open the centre before the queenside is developed. } *

[Line "Tal Variation: 4...Qb6, 4...a6 and 4...c5"]
[Section "3...Bf5 4.h4: the Tal Variation (second weapon)"]
[Summary "Black sidesteps the h-pawn question with a useful move. White plays g4 and h5 anyway and reaches the French structure with extra space, or takes on c5."]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 Bf5 4. h4 Qb6 { The queen eyes b2 and d4. The idea is to answer g4 with ...Bd7 and then
hit the pawn chain with ...h5 and ...c5, arguing that White's pawns have gone too far. }
( 4... a6 { The third most popular move in elite online games: a pure waiting move that prepares ...c5 and keeps
the bishop's options. } 5. g4 Bd7 6. h5 { Again the French structure with extra space. Black continues ...c5,
and ...a6 turns out to be only moderately useful. } )
( 4... c5 { Striking at d4 with the bishop already out. } 5. dxc5 Nc6 { 5...e6 still loses the bishop to g4. }
6. Bb5 { White pins the knight, holds the pawn with Be3 or b4 and keeps g4 in reserve to gain more time. The
main reply is 6...Qc7, when 7.Nf3 e6 follows. } )
5. g4 Bd7 { The bishop must go all the way back: 5...Be4 6.f3 Bg6 7.h5 traps it, as after 4...e6. }
6. h5 { Stops ...h5, which would undermine g4 and win f5 for a knight. }
6... e6 7. f4 c5 8. c3 { **Plan:** the structure of the 4...h6 line, with one difference in White's favour: the
h-pawn can go on to h6. White continues Nf3 and Na3-c2, covering d4 and b2.

**Verdict:** White has the space, and the queen's sortie has not changed the assessment. } *
